  </div>

  <!-- Main Editorial Content -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 sm:p-8 mb-8 prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">Why the Center "W" Rib is Critical (The Cross-Wash Problem)</h2>
    <p>
      In a standard simple V-valley (flat sheet bent down the center), rainwater flowing down Roof Plane A enters the valley at high speed. During summer cloudbursts or on unequal pitch roofs (e.g., a steep 8/12 roof intersecting a gentle 4/12 roof), high-velocity runoff shoots across the valley bottom and washes 6 to 10 inches <strong>up under the opposite panel edges</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <strong>W-profile valley</strong> features a continuous 1-inch high vertical triangular rib formed down the exact center axis. This center rib acts as an impenetrable wave-break: water hitting the center rib from either roof plane is deflected straight down the slope toward the gutter, preventing any cross-wash water from reaching opposite side panels.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">The 4-Layer Watertight Valley Assembly</h2>
    <ol class="space-y-3 not-prose my-4">
      <li class="p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">Layer 1: 36-Inch Self-Adhering Ice &amp; Water Shield</strong>
        Roll a continuous 36-inch wide strip of high-temperature rubberized asphalt membrane directly onto the plywood deck down the center of the valley before installing underlayment.
      </li>
      <li class="p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">Layer 2: W-Valley Metal Flashing with Edge Hem Folds</strong>
        Install 24-inch wide, 26-gauge pre-finished W-valley metal. Both outer edges must be hemmed 1/2 inch under to add stiffness and hook into cleat clips. Overlap upper valley sheets over lower sheets by at least 8 inches, sealed with two rows of butyl tape.
      </li>
      <li class="p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">Layer 3: Expandable Expanding Foam Closures</strong>
        Adhere expandable impregnated foam closure tape (like Emseal or Expanding Foam) along both sides of the valley, set back 1 inch from the panel cut line.
      </li>
      <li class="p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">Layer 4: Beveled Metal Roof Panels with Open Reveal</strong>
        Angle-cut roof panels parallel to the valley centerline, maintaining an open water channel reveal of 4 to 6 inches (2 to 3 inches on each side of the center rib).
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <!-- Jobsite Field Note -->
  <FieldNote
    author="David Miller, PE"
    role="Structural & Civil Engineering Consultant"
    experience="20+ Years Field Experience"
    avatarBg="bg-blue-100 text-blue-800"
    initials="DM"
    title="The Golden Rule: Never Drive a Screw Through the Valley Pan"
    note="Amateur installers cut their metal panels, lay them over the valley flashing, and drive screws straight down through the panel into the metal valley trough. Never drive a fastener through the water-carrying valley pan! A fastener driven 4 inches from the valley center will see thousands of gallons of concentrated rushing stormwater every rainstorm; even an EPDM rubber washer will leak within 3 years under continuous hydraulic head pressure. Always secure valley flashing strictly at its outermost 1-inch hemmed edges using low-profile pancake-head screws or hidden sheet metal hold-down cleats. Fasten your roof panels back at the foam closure line, at least 8 inches away from the center of the valley."
  />

  <!-- Costly Mistakes -->
  <CostlyMistakes
    tradeName="Metal Roof Valley Flashing"
    mistakes={[
      {
        number: 1,
        title: "Closing the Valley Cut Too Tight (&lt; 2-Inch Reveal)",
        costImpact: "$2,800 (Ice Damming & Pine Needle Dams)",
        description: "Butting metal panels tightly together down the center valley creates a debris dam that catches pine needles, oak leaves, and slushy ice, backing water up under the roof.",
        solution: "Maintain a minimum 4-inch to 6-inch open reveal down the valley so leaves, pine needles, and winter ice flows discharge freely into gutters."
      },
      {
        number: 2,
        title: "Overlapping Valley Sheets Less Than 8 Inches",
        costImpact: "$1,800 (Capillary Action Under-Seam Leaks)",
        description: "On long valleys requiring two pieces of flashing, a short 3-inch or 4-inch lap allows water to wick upward through surface tension and seep into the attic deck.",
        solution: "Overlap joints by at least 8 to 10 inches and seal with two continuous beads of neutral-cure non-skinning butyl tape between the metal sheets."
      },
      {
        number: 3,
        title: "Using Acid-Cure Silicone Caulk on Galvalume Flashing",
        costImpact: "$950 (Chemical Etching & Premature Flange Rust)",
        description: "Standard acetoxy silicone caulk smells like vinegar and releases acetic acid while curing, eating away the protective zinc-aluminum coating on steel roofing.",
        solution: "Use only 100% neutral-cure polyurethane (like NP-1 or Geocel 2300) or high-grade non-skinning butyl sealants approved for metal roofing."
      }
    ]}
  />

